{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>A covenant of salt forever it is before YHWH.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): a covenant is cut, for the place of salt is cut off, as it is said (Jeremiah 17:6): ״ארץ מלחה ולא תשב״." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: ״ברית מלח עולם היא״ — it means that the covenant is salt of the world, that is, the preservation of the flesh. And the essence of salt is water, and by the power of the sun that governs it, it becomes salt; if so, there is within the power of salt water and fire, and likewise the covenant is comprised of the attribute of mercy and the attribute of judgment. And this is the secret of (Genesis 36:39) ״בת מי זהב״. And Scripture explains to us that this covenant is the salt of the world, that is, the preservation of the world, for through it the world is sustained and is cut off — just as the place where salt is made is cut off and no plant will grow in it, as it is written (Jeremiah 17:6): ״ארץ מלחה ולא תשב״, and it is written (Deuteronomy 29:22): ״גפרית ומלח שרפה כל ארצה״. And it also preserves the thing, as in salting. And behold, this is one matter and its opposite, one included within the other, and the actions differ, but the principle is one." "<b>And know</b> that in these two adjacent pericopes the laws of the gifts of the priest and the Levite are explained. And in these pericopes the speech came to Aaron alone, in the expression וידבר and in the expression ויאמר. The statutes of the terumah and the rest of the gifts — which are in total twenty-four — that belong to the house of Aaron are stated in the expression וידבר; the statutes of the tithe to the house of Levi are stated in the expression ויאמר. Since you already know that the attribute of the priest is the Written Torah, and the attribute of the Levite is the Oral Torah, therefore the Written Torah merited for him twenty-four priestly gifts, corresponding to the twenty-four books." "<b>And you will find</b> in the census of the Levites that concerning them it is stated ״על פי HaShem״ — something you will not find in the census of the children of Israel. And this alludes to the attribute that is the Oral Torah, for our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded (Exodus 34:27): ״כי על פי הדברים האלה כרתי אתך ברית״ — the Holy One, blessed be He, did not make a covenant with Israel except for the sake of the Oral Torah. And open the eyes of your heart and you will see how Scripture explained that the inheritance of the priest is אני (I), from what it says (Numbers 18:20): ״אני חלקך ונחלתך״, and it is the attribute of mercy in this place, as in (Exodus 6:5): ״וגם אני שמעתי״. And the inheritance of the Levite is the tithe, which is an allusion to the tenth part. And from this it says (Numbers 18:21): ״ולבני לוי הנה נתתי כל מעשר בישראל לנחלה״. And so you will find Levi son of Jacob, who was tenth among the sons, for it is written (Genesis 28:22): ״וכל אשר תתן לי עשר אעשרנו לך״ — even from his sons he gave a tithe to the Holy One, blessed be He. And the tenth is holy, as in (I Chronicles 23:13): ״ויבדל אהרן להקדישו קדש קדשים״. And the way of a shepherd, when he brings his flock into the pen and wishes to count them, he takes them out one by one; and the one that enters last exits first. So too the tribes, when he began to count from Benjamin, who is the last, Levi would be tenth among the sons." "<b>And so</b> you will find in Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer, chapter 37: Jacob did not tithe except retroactively — he began from Benjamin, who was in his mother's womb, and Levi arose as holy to HaShem, and concerning him Scripture says (Leviticus 27:32): ״העשירי יהיה קדש לHaShem״. The angel Michael descended and took Levi and brought him up before the Throne of Glory. He said before Him: Master of the Universe, this is his lot and this is your tithe. He stretched out His right hand and blessed him, that the sons of Levi would serve before Him on earth like the ministering angels in heaven. Michael said before the Holy One, blessed be He: Master of the Universe, does one not give sustenance to the servants of the king? Therefore He gave to the sons of Levi every holy offering that ascends for His name, as it is said (Deuteronomy 18:1): ״אשי HaShem ונחלתו יאכלון״. Thus far in the Pirkei." "<b>And behold.</b> The Levite is sanctified, but the priest is more sanctified than he, and the Levite proclaims the preeminence of the priest in that he is obligated to give him the tithe of the tithe, for the Levites are subjugated to the priests, as it is written (Numbers 8:19): ״ואתנה את הלוים נתונים לאהרן ולבניו״." "<b>Observe now</b> and you will see how Scripture mentions of the priest 'she' (היא), and of the Levite 'he' (הוא) — in each one it mentions the opposite of its attribute. Of the priest it says, 'an everlasting covenant of salt' — <b>she is</b> (היא), and of the Levite it says, <b>he is</b> (הוא) — to teach you that there is no separation among the attributes but rather joining and complete unity, and this one is included within that one. And because of this it mentions here the expression היא, which indicates the attribute of judgment from one side, in its saying היא, and indicates the attribute of mercy, which is the first half of the unique name ה׳, and the word הוא that it mentions regarding the Levite indicates the attribute of mercy from one side, in its saying הוא in masculine form, and from another side indicates the attribute of judgment, in that it includes the latter half of the Name, which is the attribute of judgment — to teach you that the one is included within its companion. And a parallel to this you will find in our father Jacob and King David, peace be upon them, in whom each one's opposite attribute is mentioned: Jacob, of whom it is written (Micah 7:20): ״תתן אמת ליעקב״ — says (Genesis 48:15): ״האלהים הרועה אותי״; and David, peace be upon him, of whom it is written (Psalms 72:1): ״משפטיך למלך תן״ — says (Psalms 23:1): ״ה' רועי לא אחסר״. And so each one expressed the matter of sustenance in the opposite of his attribute."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {4 "Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer ch. 37 cited by chapter number; the order and some details of the midrash as quoted here differ from standard printed editions of PdRE.", 6 "The comment presupposes the surrounding verse (Numbers 18:19), where 'she is' (היא) refers to the covenant of salt given to the priest and 'he is' (הוא) in v. 21 refers to the tithe given to the Levite; the grammatical antecedents are implicit throughout."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:34:11.686716655Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}